     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What is Qi certification and why do OEM importers need it?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Qi certification is the global wireless charging standard managed by the Wireless Power Consortium (WPC). It verifies your product meets safety, efficiency (≥75% minimum), and interoperability standards. Without certification, you cannot legally use the Qi logo, sell on Amazon (which actively delists uncertified wireless chargers), or access major retail channels. Qi2 certification adds mandatory MPP (Magnetic Power Profile) alignment testing and FOD (Foreign Object Detection) verification. As of February 2026, 637 products are Qi2-certified, with 69.62% at the 25W Qi2.2 tier. Non-certified products also risk CE/FCC compliance issues, the Qi logo is treated as a safety compliance indicator by EU and US customs authorities."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What is the difference between Qi, Qi2, and Qi2.2 certification?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Qi (original): 5W baseline, no magnetic alignment, obsolete for new 2026 OEM development. Qi2 (15W): introduces MPP magnetic alignment with N52H neodymium magnets, requires FOD verification. Qi2.2 (25W, released July 2025): doubles power to 25W, requires 30W+ USB-C PD adapter, active cooling, upgraded coil modules, and new thermal management ICs handling 67% more heat. Qi2.2 is backward compatible with Qi2 and Qi devices. As of February 2026, 69.62% of new WPC certifications are at Qi2.2 25W, making 15W Qi2 face rapid margin compression. For new OEM products in 2026: Qi2 (15W) is the minimum viable tier; Qi2.2 (25W) is the recommended standard."
     }
    },
